      "card_ids": ["C08"],
      "claim": "Pitolisant, a piperidine-containing CNS drug, showed good oral absorption and brain penetration in rodent in vivo studies, supporting the CNS access of basic amine motifs.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C01"],
      "claim": "Biperiden, a basic amine drug with a piperidine ring, crosses the blood-brain barrier with a brain-to-plasma ratio of 7-12, demonstrating that such cationic amines can achieve meaningful CNS exposure.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C09"],
      "claim": "A polar fluorinated alcohol metabolite showed low brain uptake after intravenous injection, but this compound lacks the lipophilic azepane and guanidine motifs of the query and is weakly transferable.",
      "evidence_role": "contradictory"
    }
  ],
  "confidence": "moderate",
  "decision_summary": "The most transferable evidence is from NY0128, a guanidine-alkyl-lipophilic compound that directly demonstrated brain penetration and brain accumulation in rats. Additional CNS-penetrant basic amine drugs support the ability of such motifs to cross the BBB. The single negative record is from a structurally dissimilar polar alcohol metabolite and is outweighed. The query's guanidine-ethyl-azepane scaffold is predicted to achieve meaningful CNS access.",
  "evidence_gaps": [
    "No direct brain or plasma pharmacokinetic data exist for the exact query molecule.",
    "The query is likely dicationic at physiological pH, and its passive permeability relative to the more lipophilic NY0128 is uncertain.",
